<article class="env-proposition" id="06RD">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06RD">Proposition <span data-tag="06RD">4.7.4.22</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ denote the homotopy category of the heart $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\heartsuit }$. Then the canonical map $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\heartsuit } \rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0)$ is a trivial Kan fibration. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Fix an integer $m \geq 0$; we wish to show that every lifting problem 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^{m} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\heartsuit } \ar [d] \\ \Delta ^{m} \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 ). } \]
</div>
<p>
       For $m \leq 1$, this is immediate (since the right vertical map is bijective on vertices and surjective on edges). For $m = 2$, it follows from the definition of the composition law on the homotopy category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ (see Notation <a data-tag="0044" href="/tag/0044">1.4.4.3</a>). For $m \geq 3$, it follows from the characterization of discrete objects given in Remark <a data-tag="06RC" href="/tag/06RC">4.7.4.20</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
